{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[node_content]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This storage-only consumer must delegate load/store using node_path\n without resolving contents. Domain requires the callback, but invoking\n this guard would violate the no-content-read invariant of the test.\n Other graph fixtures exercise content resolution."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[node_content]` is valid.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced `:::{cite} habeas:coverage` block placed on an explicitly declared function with a body. The explanation is in fenced commentary, which the built-in requires.\n- **Substance:** The reason is specific. `Domain` requires the `node_content` callback. `StoredGraph` is a storage-only fixture, so its `node_content` is a deliberate `panic!` guard: \"storage operations must not read graph contents\". The function is never meant to run, so its zero execution is acceptable. The commentary also says other fixtures exercise content resolution.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - `StoredGraph`'s `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` only call `self.0.load` and `self.0.store` with `self.node_path(node)`. They never touch contents.\n  - `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` uses only these methods and `node_path`.\n  - `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` runs a `StoredReview` criterion through `Validator`. That criterion's `execute` ignores its context, so no dependencies are recorded and nothing reads contents.\n- **Other fixtures:** The other graph fixtures with real `node_content` implementations (`crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs`, `validation.rs` and `contracts.rs`) support the claim that content resolution is exercised elsewhere.\n- **Redundancy:** The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to a cited requirement, so the citation is not redundant. It is the only citation on the item.\n\nI found no gap.",
